        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The 45 m² exemption is Class 3A of the Planning and Development (Exempted Development (Act of 2000)) (No. 3) Regulations 2026, S.I. No. 340 of 2026, which came into operation on 27 July 2026. It allows a detached home of between 32 and 45 square metres to be built in the rear garden of the house you live in without a planning application, provided the unit is linked to the services of the principal house and every condition is met, including notifying the planning authority at least 14 days before work starts. Works must be commenced and completed by 31 December 2030." }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How much can a 45 m² back-garden modular home in Ireland cost?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Irish-built back-garden modular homes typically range from €110,000 (basic 2-bed shell) to €180,000 (turnkey luxury) for a 32 to 45 square metre unit. Imported steel-frame and expandable container homes from Henan Province, supplied and installed by Ériu Modular Homes with full Irish Building Regulations compliance, are typically 25 to 45 percent below Irish-built pricing for an equivalent specification. Pricing is project-specific to your site, tier and finish choices." }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I rent out my back-garden modular home?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes, on a long-term basis. The RTB has stated that renting a qualifying Class 3A unit does not come under its remit and does not need to be registered with the RTB. Short-term letting is not permitted, and the unit must be occupied in conjunction with the main house. Plan on the rent being taxable: Revenue's Rent-a-Room guidance states that a self-contained unit not attached to the property cannot qualify for the relief. The yield depends on your all-in installed cost, so we model it against a real quote rather than quote a headline percentage. Confirm the tax treatment with your accountant." }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does a 45 m² back-garden home need planning permission?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Under Class 3A, in force since 27 July 2026, no planning application is needed, provided every condition is met: 32 to 45 square metres, detached, in the rear garden, linked to the services of the principal house, within the height limits, and notified to the planning authority at least 14 days before work starts. However, planning exemption is not building regulations exemption. Every habitable structure in Ireland must still meet the Irish Building Regulations (TGD Parts A to M) and is subject to BER assessment. The Society of Chartered Surveyors Ireland (SCSI) and the Institute of Professional Auctioneers and Valuers (IPAV) have both warned that buyers often confuse the two. Ériu Modular Homes supplies units fully specified for Irish Building Regulations compliance, with the written compliance pack accompanying every order." }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What conditions must a back-garden modular home meet to qualify for the exemption?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Class 3A requires, among other conditions: the unit is detached and in the rear garden; floor area is between 32 and 45 square metres; the principal house is the owner's sole or main residence when work begins; services are linked to the principal house; height does not exceed 4 metres with a pitched tiled or slated roof, or 3 metres for any other roof; enough private garden space is kept; the planning authority is notified at least 14 days before work starts; and the unit is occupied only with the main house, is not sold or subdivided separately and is not used for short-term letting. Protected Structures and other site factors can still take a project outside the exemption. Each Ériu Modular Homes project includes a site eligibility check against the full conditions in S.I. No. 340 of 2026." }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How will a back-garden modular home affect my home insurance and mortgage?",
        "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Home insurance must be updated to declare the new structure, failing to do so can invalidate cover on the principal house. Most Irish insurers will quote an uplifted premium rather than require a separate policy. On mortgages, most main Irish lenders will not currently extend a mortgage on a property that contains a second self-contained dwelling on the same title, which has resale implications. Some homeowners use chattel mortgages, personal loans or credit union finance to fund the modular unit specifically to avoid affecting the principal house mortgage. Ériu Modular Homes provides a written LPT, insurance and mortgage briefing pack with every quote." }
